mcu: peel the ROM cases off the instruction-fetch path

Every instruction fetch calls MCU_Read(), which decodes the page with a switch
and, for page 0, a chain of range tests, in order to reach one of two plain
array indexings that have no side effects.  Code lives in ROM, so handle those
two cases in the header where they inline into MCU_ReadCodeAdvance() and the
call disappears.

Measured on the mk2 benchmark (32 PCM slots, placeholder ROMs, callgrind Ir):
7,864,728,794 -> 7,836,682,972, -0.36%.  That understates the effect: with
placeholder ROMs the program counter sweeps the whole 64 KiB of page 0, so only
about 56% of fetches land in ROM1.  Real firmware fetches from ROM essentially
always.

Proof: patches/tests/mcu_code_fetch_equivalence.cpp links the real MCU_Read()
against side-effect-recording stubs and checks all 2^20 (cp, pc) pairs for all
nine romsets.

--- a/src/backend/mcu.h
+++ b/src/backend/mcu.h
@@ -322,8 +322,35 @@
     return ((uint32_t)page << 16) + address;
 }
 
+// Instruction fetch. Every fetch goes through MCU_Read(), whose page decode is
+// a switch plus, for page 0, a chain of range tests -- all of it to reach one
+// of two array indexings that carry no side effects. Code lives in ROM, so peel
+// those two cases off in the header where they inline into the fetch.
+//
+// These reproduce MCU_Read()'s cases exactly:
+//
+//   page 0, address < 0x8000     ->  rom1[address & 0x7fff]
+//   pages 1..4                   ->  rom2[address_rom & rom2_mask]
+//
+// With address = (cp << 16) + pc and cp <= 4 the page is cp, the low 16 bits
+// are pc, and bit 0x80000 (the only thing that alters address_rom) is clear.
+// Neither case reads or writes anything but the ROM array, so declining to
+// call MCU_Read() cannot lose an access side effect. Anything else -- I/O
+// space, RAM, SRAM, the unmapped pages -- falls through to MCU_Read() as
+// before. See patches/tests/mcu_code_fetch_equivalence.cpp.
 inline uint8_t MCU_ReadCode(mcu_t& mcu) {
-    return MCU_Read(mcu, MCU_GetAddress(mcu.cp, mcu.pc));
+    const uint8_t  cp = mcu.cp;
+    const uint16_t pc = mcu.pc;
+    if (cp == 0)
+    {
+        if (!(pc & 0x8000))
+            return mcu.rom1[pc];
+    }
+    else if (cp <= 4)
+    {
+        return mcu.rom2[(((uint32_t)cp << 16) | pc) & 0x3ffffu & mcu.rom2_mask];
+    }
+    return MCU_Read(mcu, MCU_GetAddress(cp, pc));
 }
 
 inline uint8_t MCU_ReadCodeAdvance(mcu_t& mcu) {
